description:
src/methods/mod.rs:213-221:
```
pub(crate) fn build_request(
    method: &str,
    args: serde_json::Value,
    using: &[&str],
) -> jmap_types::JmapRequest {
    let using_vec: Vec<String> = using.iter().map(|&s| s.to_owned()).collect();
    let invocation: jmap_types::Invocation = (method.to_owned(), args, CALL_ID.to_owned());
    jmap_types::JmapRequest::new(using_vec, vec![invocation], None)
}
```

Two heap allocs per request that look avoidable:
- `using_vec`: copies the static USING_CALENDARS `&[&str]` into a fresh Vec<String> on every call. The capability URI strings are 'static.
- `CALL_ID.to_owned()`: "r1" allocated freshly each call.

If JmapRequest::new accepts `Vec<String>` and `Invocation = (String, Value, String)` (which it does — checked jmap-types/src/wire.rs:10), the allocations are forced by jmap-types' API. So this is partially a downstream API constraint, not a code-quality issue here.

Worth noting in case future-self looks at the hot path. NOT independently actionable in this crate without changing the foundation type crate jmap-types. Filing as a Section 2 efficiency note for future reference. Probably don't act on this.
